    "result_text": "Green. **BUILD SUCCESS**, all 3 tests pass (0 failures/errors).\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded optional owner `email` \u2014 validated when present, stored and returned lower-cased \u2014 with minimal structural impact by adding small, cohesive units rather than growing existing methods.\n\n**Files touched (6):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added an optional `email` (`type: string, format: email`) to the `OwnerFields` schema. Because `Owner` is `allOf OwnerFields`, both the generated request DTO (`OwnerFieldsDto`) and response DTO (`OwnerDto`) gain `email`, and MapStruct auto-maps it by name in both directions \u2014 so no mapper change was needed.\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 new `email` column plus a getter and a setter that lower-cases (`email.toLowerCase()`). This is the single normalization point, so every write path (create via mapper, update via `ApplyOwner`) stores it lower-cased.\n3. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** / **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 added the `email` column (the test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema is explicit) and appended `NULL` to the positional seed inserts. The default `h2` profile needs no change (`ddl-auto=update` + column-listed inserts).\n4. **`BuildOwner.java`** (POST path, which does not run `@Valid`) \u2014 rejects a syntactically invalid email by throwing the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException`, reusing its 400 handler.\n5. **`ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT path) \u2014 one line copying `email` so updates persist it; format validation there comes for free from the DTO's `@Email` via the existing `ValidateOwner` `@Valid` step.\n\nEvery changed function stayed simple: the `Owner` accessors are trivial, the `BuildOwner` addition is a single guard, and `ApplyOwner` grew by one line in a one-method class. No new controller/exception/handler classes or YAML wiring were introduced.",
